  "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$",
